## Chapter 5 — Capture

> “Institutions rarely become authoritarian overnight. They become indispensable first.”

The word capture is often used casually.

Corporations capture regulators.

Political parties capture agencies.

Special interests capture legislatures.

Media captures public attention.

Within this framework, however, capture has a more precise meaning.

Capture occurs when a mediator becomes structurally necessary.

Not influential.

Necessary.

The distinction matters.

Influence can be resisted.

Necessity cannot.

### The Mediator

Every institution contains mediators.

A mediator reduces complexity.

Courts mediate disputes.

Banks mediate exchange.

Governments mediate law.

Teachers mediate knowledge.

Journalists mediate information.

Algorithms mediate attention.

Most mediation is beneficial.

Civilizations depend upon it.

Problems emerge only when mediation quietly transforms into dependency.

### From Option to Requirement

Capture rarely begins with coercion.

It begins with convenience.

An institution performs a task more efficiently than alternatives.

People adopt it voluntarily.

Over time, alternatives disappear.

Eventually the question changes.

Instead of asking,

> "Should we use this?" people begin asking,

> "How could we function without it?"

That transition marks the beginning of structural capture.

The mediator is no longer one pathway among many.

It has become the pathway.

### Gatekeepers

Every captured system develops gatekeepers.

Gatekeepers decide:

Who enters.

Who leaves.

Who qualifies.

Who speaks.

Who receives.

Who waits.

Sometimes gatekeepers are human.

Sometimes they are bureaucratic.

Sometimes they are technological.

Sometimes they are cultural.

The mechanism matters more than the medium.

### Invisible Capture

Successful capture often produces no obvious tyrant.

Instead it produces friction.

Small delays.

Additional approvals.

Required accounts.

Mandatory registrations.

Exclusive certifications.

Security clearances.

Compliance reviews.

Individually, each requirement appears reasonable.

Collectively, they redefine participation.

The institution no longer facilitates action.

It authorizes action.

### Bypass Cost

Every gatekeeper increases bypass cost.

Bypass cost measures the practical difficulty of accomplishing a task without the approved intermediary.

Healthy institutions tolerate alternative pathways.

Captured institutions steadily eliminate them.

Formal freedom may remain.

Practical freedom declines.

A system may legally permit alternatives while making those alternatives economically, administratively, technologically, or politically impossible.

Capture often advances through this gap between formal possibility and practical accessibility.

### The Capture Gradient

Capture is rarely binary.

Institutions usually move along a continuum.

Open access.

Preferred access.

Expected access.

Required access.

Exclusive access.

Each transition appears modest.

The cumulative effect can be profound.

The final stage often appears inevitable only because earlier transitions have already removed meaningful alternatives.

### Self-Reinforcing Systems

Capture tends to reproduce itself.

The more necessary an institution becomes, the more resources flow toward it.

The more resources it receives,

the more indispensable it becomes.

This feedback loop increases institutional inertia.

Correction becomes increasingly expensive.

Eventually reform itself appears destabilizing because the institution has become integrated into every surrounding system.

### Capture Without Conspiracy

Capture does not require malicious actors.

No coordinated plan is necessary.

Individuals pursuing reasonable goals can collectively produce unreasonable structures.

Efficiency.

Security.

Professionalization.

Risk reduction.

Standardization.

Optimization.

Each objective may be defensible.

Their cumulative effect may nevertheless reduce replaceability and increase dependence.

For this reason, this framework evaluates structural outcomes rather than presumed intentions.

Intent may matter.

Structure persists.

### Measuring Capture

Several recurring questions guide analysis.

- Has the number of alternative pathways increased or decreased?
- Has review become easier or more difficult?
- Has authority become more centralized or more distributed?
- Has bypass cost increased?
- Has replaceability declined?
- Has scheduled reassessment disappeared?

These questions are descriptive.

They do not assume good faith.

They do not assume bad faith.

They measure institutional trajectory.

### Closing Principle

Capture is not defined by power alone.

Power exists in every organized society.

Capture begins when power becomes increasingly difficult to bypass, increasingly difficult to review, and increasingly difficult to replace.

The transition is usually gradual.

Its consequences are often recognized only after alternatives have quietly disappeared.

